Let Unity’s physics system control the motion. For a physics-driven ball, set its velocity once or update it in FixedUpdate:

```csharp
public Rigidbody2D rb;
public float speed = 20f;
public Vector2 direction = Vector2.right;

void Start()
{
    rb.velocity = direction.normalized * speed;
}
```

If direction changes continuously, assign `rb.velocity` in `FixedUpdate`. Avoid multiplying velocity by `Time.deltaTime`; velocity is already measured in units per second. If you need position-based movement instead, call `rb.MovePosition(rb.position + movement * Time.fixedDeltaTime)` from `FixedUpdate`. Keep Continuous collision detection enabled for the fast-moving ball.

Make the ball’s Collider2D a trigger, then handle `OnTriggerEnter2D`. A trigger reports overlap without producing a physical bounce:

```csharp
void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
{
    if (other.CompareTag("Wall"))
    {
        // Record score, play effects, etc.
        Destroy(gameObject);
    }
}
```

Keep the Rigidbody2D on the ball so Unity performs continuous trigger detection, and confirm the ball and wall layers interact in the Physics 2D matrix. For effects that must outlive the ball, spawn them before destroying it. If balls are created frequently, consider deactivating and returning them to an object pool instead of calling `Destroy` each time.
